// Cross-variant capability parity for the Orca X product family.
//
// Why this exists separately from the two manifests beside it: reliability-gates.jsonc disciplines
// *claims* (a gate may not assert protection without a real test and a passed run), and
// durability-contracts.jsonc answers *what a write owes* if its state is lost. Neither asks the
// question this one does -- when a capability improves on one variant, does the sibling variant
// inherit it, and if not, why not?
//
// That question needs its own registry because nobody was asking it. Measured 2026-09-08: 195
// fork-local commits since the upstream fork point e4e54a17d, 367 files changed, and *zero* of them
// touch mobile/. The mobile app is not behind because it is primitive -- it is 743 source files and
// 111k lines with a stronger transport layer than the desktop's. It is behind because every
// improvement landed on the desktop side of a wire that never learned to describe it.
//
// Parity here is SEMANTIC, never file-based. "Desktop changed file X so mobile must change file Y"
// is not a rule this gate can state or would want to. A capability may be shared code, a shared
// protocol, a different presentation of one invariant, or genuinely inapplicable. What the gate
// refuses is the fifth case: nobody checked.
//
// dispositions:
//   shared-already   One implementation serves both variants, or one mirrors the other under a test
//                    that fails when they drift. Requires `evidence`.
//   ported           The desktop behaviour now exists in mobile. Requires `evidence`.
//   equivalent       Mobile expresses the same user capability and invariant through a different
//                    presentation. Requires `evidence` AND `invariant`.
//   desktop-only     The capability cannot exist on mobile by nature. Requires `reason`.
//   not-applicable   Mobile has no such surface. Requires `reason`.
//   superseded       Mobile already solves this, at least as well. Requires `reason`.
//   blocked          Real gap with a named external blocker. Requires `reason` and `owner`.
//   measured-open    Investigated, gap named, nothing external blocking it -- it just is not built.
//                    Requires `evidence` (what was measured) AND `reason` (what remains and what
//                    closes it). Ratcheted separately from unknown.
//   unknown          Nobody has looked yet. Ratcheted -- see unknownPolicy.
//
// Why unknown and measured-open are two states and not one: they need different actions. An unknown
// needs someone to investigate; a measured-open gap needs someone to implement. Collapsing them
// understates what has been learned and hides which of the two a capability is waiting on. The
// hazard that comes with splitting them is relabelling -- calling a row "measured" and booking it as
// progress -- so the gate counts BOTH lists in one unresolved total, and that total only falls when
// a capability is genuinely resolved. Knowing more is not the same as having built it.
//
// severity is optional and drawn from `severities`. An unresolved capability without one counts as
// P0 in the release gate: forgetting to triage can only make the gate stricter.
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "updatedAt": "2026-09-08",
  "policy": {
    "variants": ["desktop", "mobile"],
    "dispositions": [
      "shared-already",
      "ported",
      "equivalent",
      "desktop-only",
      "not-applicable",
      "superseded",
      "blocked",
      "measured-open",
      "unknown"
    ],
    "evidenceRequiredFor": ["shared-already", "ported", "equivalent", "measured-open"],
    "reasonRequiredFor": ["desktop-only", "not-applicable", "superseded", "blocked", "measured-open"],
    "invariantRequiredFor": ["equivalent"],
    // The two kinds of debt. Summed for the unresolved total the gate prints on every run.
    "unresolvedDispositions": ["unknown", "measured-open"],
    "severities": ["P0", "P1", "P2", "P3"],
    // Ratcheted, not reported: config/variant-parity-unknown-baseline.txt and
    // config/variant-parity-measured-open-baseline.txt grandfather the capabilities that predate
    // each gate and may only shrink. Existing debt stays visible while a NEW entry in either state
    // fails. "There were already some" is not an argument a future change can make.
    "unknownPolicy": "ratchet",
    "measuredOpenPolicy": "ratchet"
  },
  "capabilities": [
    {
      "id": "agents.catalog-membership",
      "title": "Both variants offer the same set of agents, in the same order",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/tasks/mobile-agent-catalog.test.ts"],
      "notes": "Mobile mirrors src/shared/tui-agent-selection.ts and the test reads the desktop source across the project boundary to assert it. Measured in parity at 36 agents on 2026-09-08."
    },
    {
      "id": "agents.display-label",
      "title": "An agent carries the same user-facing name on both variants",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/tasks/mobile-agent-catalog.test.ts"],
      "notes": "The fork renamed the Codex launch row to ChatGPT in the renderer catalog alone, so the same agent had two names in one product. The membership test could not see it: it compared order and set, never labels. Widened 2026-09-08, which is the drift this registry was opened on. Desktop still labels Codex as 'Codex' on non-launch surfaces (notifications, CLI, usage provider); that is a deliberate scoping decision by fe30a7426, not drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.launch-profile-offer",
      "title": "Both variants offer the same set of launch profiles, from one composer",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/session/mobile-launch-profile-parity.test.ts"],
      "notes": "Desktop composed agents, shells and user-declared profiles; mobile answered the same question from its own agents-only list, so a declared profile existed on one variant only. The composer now lives in src/shared/terminal-launch-profiles.ts and both variants read it, so a new agent reaches both from one edit. No wire change was needed: session.tabs.createTerminal already carried command, env, delivery and an idempotency key over a required worktree, and terminalLaunchProfiles already rode in the settings payload mobile fetches. Mobile deliberately drops shell rows (it has its own plain-terminal row and no Windows shell menu) and keeps the diff-notes list agents-only (a declared ssh session cannot receive a prompt)."
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.launch-profile-identity",
      "title": "A terminal can say which launch profile it came from, and tell recorded from inferred",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Distinct from terminal.launch-profile-offer, which is closed: this is what an ALREADY RUNNING terminal can say about its own origin, and mobile still infers agent identity from a title glyph. src/shared/terminal-launch-profile-identity.ts is pure TypeScript with type-only imports, so it is portable as-is. The earlier note here claimed mobile was blocked on wire aperture; that was measured on the registry module rather than the capability and was wrong -- the launch verb was reachable all along. What is genuinely unmeasured is whether the recorded/inferred distinction survives to a mobile session tab."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.lifecycle-vocabulary",
      "title": "The user can tell reconnect from resume from restart from restore-state-only",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Measured 2026-09-08, and narrower than the title implies. Desktop's actual user-facing vocabulary is two values -- 'restored' and 'resume-unavailable' (session-restored-banner-pane-state.ts) -- driven by an agentResumeUnavailable flag whose complete inventory is main/ipc/pty.ts, preload and renderer, with ZERO references under src/main/runtime/rpc or mobile/. That half is genuinely absent from the wire and needs an additive negotiated capability, unlike restoredUnconfirmed which was already there and is now consumed. sessionBoundary is separately not a mobile concern: mobile has no `state === 'done'` branch in any non-test source, so there is no completion consumer to mislead. Kept unknown rather than reclassified because this manifest has no disposition for a measured-but-open gap with no external blocker; that is a taxonomy gap, not an unexamined capability."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.unconfirmed-activity-is-not-presented-as-live",
      "title": "A row the host could not confirm is not shown as a working agent",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/worktree/agent-row-unconfirmed-provenance.test.ts"],
      "notes": "restoredUnconfirmed rides on RuntimeWorktreeAgentRow and says the host hydrated a nonterminal row from disk with no live hook since. Mobile dropped it -- it was not in agentDotState's Pick -- so a turn that may have ended hours ago rendered as Working until a 30-minute clock expired on a timestamp that was never evidence of liveness. Mobile now uses the desktop's own isFreshNonDoneAgentStatus instead of a local mirror; the mirror is how the clause went missing, having copied the timeout and not the condition. The list equality was extended to compare the field, because a field the renderer reads must be one the change check sees."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.background-does-not-stop-remote-work",
      "title": "Backgrounding the control client does not stop host-owned work",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile already holds this: the host PTY outlives the app, and mobile re-subscribes on foreground (mobile/src/terminal/terminal-foreground-recovery.ts). The webview backing store dies, not the remote work. Nothing to port; what is missing is a proof, tracked under session.lifecycle-vocabulary."
    },
    {
      "id": "transport.reconnect-and-command-deduplication",
      "title": "A dropped connection recovers without duplicating commands",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile's transport is ahead of the desktop's here and was never a fork target: E2EE v2, relay/direct upgrade with hysteresis, a reconnect controller, a resume director, a session-liveness watchdog, and request-single-flight. Porting desktop semantics onto this would be a regression."
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace.repo-and-worktree-are-distinct",
      "title": "Selecting a worktree acts on that worktree, not on the repo's primary checkout",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/worktree/workspace-list-types.ts"],
      "notes": "repoId and worktreeId are distinct fields on mobile with full lineage modelling, so the flattening risk this family exists to catch does not apply."
    },
    {
      "id": "source-control.actions",
      "title": "Source control is usable, not merely viewable",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile is at action parity, not view parity: stage, commit, push with --force-with-lease, pull, PR, branch-compare and conflict-abort all exist. No porting owed."
    },
    {
      "id": "voice.no-auto-submit",
      "title": "A transcript is never submitted to an agent without the user acting",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/app.json"],
      "notes": "Mobile streams microphone audio to the paired desktop over speech.* RPC and inserts the transcript without submitting it; no auto-submit path exists on either variant. ASR placement differs (host-local for mobile) but the safety contract is identical."
    },
    {
      "id": "voice.transcript-target-ownership",
      "title": "A transcript belongs to the input that started recording, not the one focused when it lands",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Desktop pins this (fork commits 90f53945d, 80b685f84). Mobile's equivalent is unmeasured: navigating away mid-transcription is the case that would show it."
    },
    {
      "id": "navigation.reversible-navigation-preserves-context",
      "title": "Leaving a work surface and coming back preserves the scene rather than recreating it",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Mobile screens survive navigation only because no screen sets unmountOnBlur or freezeOnBlur. The capability is real and nothing protects it, so one future flag deletes it with a green suite."
    },
    {
      "id": "drafts.user-invested-input-survives-process-death",
      "title": "Text the user composed and has not sent survives the app being killed",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Mobile has an editable file preview with dirty-draft tracking and no persistence site was found, so process death discards it silently. Needs a durability tier in config/durability-contracts.jsonc rather than a mobile-only taxonomy."
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.mutation-carries-its-own-context",
      "title": "A mobile mutation names the workspace it acts on, and is refused without one",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["src/main/runtime/mobile-rpc-allowlist.test.ts"],
      "notes": "One wire schema serves both variants. Measured 2026-09-08: the RPC method directory holds no BrowserWindow reference, and of the 40+ state-changing methods mobile can reach, all but four reject a request naming no workspace -- settings.update and ui.set are host-global by design, terminal.multiplex routes by streamId, and terminal.create leaves worktree optional and is not on any mobile path. The test enumerates by mutation verb rather than by looking for the context check, so it can find a method that lacks one."
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.command-acts-on-the-window-that-owns-the-target",
      "title": "A remote command reaches the workspace it named, not a representative one",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "Narrowed, not closed. The inbound half is measured under runtime.mutation-carries-its-own-context; what remains is the OUTBOUND half, where orca-runtime.ts notifiersFor delivers to every bound window when a target cannot be placed. That fallback is deliberate and defended in its own comment -- dropping a command silently would be worse -- and for a notification it is likely right. Unknown because nobody has checked what a mobile-initiated command looks like to the wrong window when it arrives there, not because the routing is unexamined."
    },
    {
      "id": "authority.repository-mutations-are-classified-by-stale-consequence",
      "title": "Every repository mutation a client can reach says what a stale caller does to it",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["src/main/runtime/mobile-git-mutation-consequence.test.ts"],
      "notes": "One wire schema serves both variants, so the classification is the product's, not mobile's. Each git and worktree method mobile can reach is a read or a mutation with a named protection -- compare-and-swap, guarded-by-git, converges-on-repeat, operation-id, or recoverable -- and a method in neither list fails the sweep, so a newly allowlisted mutation cannot inherit the subsystem's reputation without being looked at. The sweep's red branch is driven by a test, and the unprotected set is shrink-only."
    },
    {
      "id": "authority.stale-client-cannot-overwrite-current-state",
      "title": "A client that reconnects after missing changes adopts current state instead of publishing its own",
      "disposition": "unknown",
      "notes": "The last sentence of this note used to read 'desktop-vs-mobile git and worktree mutations are unfenced'. Measured 2026-09-08, that is wrong. Four mechanisms cover the surface: a required compare-and-swap on a ref (worktree.forceDeleteBranch/expectedHead, git.forkSync/expectedUpstream), git's own refusal of a non-fast-forward or a work-losing checkout, --force-with-lease on the force push, and clientMutationId on the creates. Every mobile-reachable git and worktree method is now classified in src/main/runtime/mobile-git-mutation-consequence.test.ts, and one comes out unprotected: git.discard destroys uncommitted changes at a path with no statement of what the caller believed was there, and mobile calls it live from two surfaces. Every other stale case is recoverable from the object graph; uncommitted work is not. Kept unknown for that one operation -- closing it needs a per-file identity the porcelain parser currently discards, which is a parser change, a hot-read-path shape change, a negotiated capability and a mixed-version matrix."
    },
    {
      "id": "product.display-name",
      "title": "Both variants present the product under its current name",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": [
        "mobile/src/diagnostics/connection-diagnostics-report.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/components/HostProtocolGate.test.ts"
      ],
      "notes": "Desktop is 'Orca X' via src/shared/product.ts. Mobile named itself 'Orca' on its icon, about screen, onboarding, diagnostics and OS permission prompts, and called the desktop 'Orca' throughout its prose. Both halves are done; doing only the first would have reproduced the split-label defect this registry opened on. The rename deliberately does not reach scheme 'orca', bundle id com.stably.orca.mobile, slug orca-mobile, or the 'orca:' storage-key prefix -- those are what deep links, the stores and already-paired devices resolve against."
    }
  ]
}
